id: pm-dqej6b
title: Scratch lifecycle 15-second wall-clock assertion flakes under default full coverage concurrency
description: "Two local full-coverage runs on 2026-09-29 failed only tests/integration/scratch-lifecycle.integration.spec.ts:132 because the entire nine-command scratch CLI lifecycle took 16166.9ms and 15517.2ms against a fixed 15000ms wall-clock assertion. The same test passed alone in 4155ms, and a full run with Vitest --maxWorkers 4 passed 9433 tests at exact 100/100/100/100. This is evidence of a load-sensitive quality gate, not proof of a product latency regression."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 2
tags[4]: "area:quality",coverage,flaky-test,performance
created_at: "2026-09-29T11:40:12.733Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-29T11:58:23.810Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 120
acceptance_criteria: Reproduce under controlled concurrency and measure the real CLI command costs; distinguish product latency from host scheduling contention without deleting or blindly increasing the performance budget; make the default full-coverage gate deterministic while preserving exact 100/100/100/100 coverage and integration lifecycle assertions.
parent: pm-doxj
risk: medium
confidence: high
severity: medium
expected_result: The scratch lifecycle performance contract identifies real CLI regressions without failing solely from unrelated parallel test load.
actual_result: Two default-worker coverage runs were red solely at the 15-second assertion; isolated and four-worker runs passed with the same production code.
component: test reliability
